+# Public JSON posts feed
+
+## Context
+
+Partner integrations want to embed our latest posts on their own sites, and
+our front-end wants the same list without an Inertia round trip. Until now
+posts were only reachable through the HTML/Inertia pages, which an external
+consumer cannot parse. A partner has no session cookie and no CSRF token, so
+whatever we expose has to be a safe, unauthenticated read.
+
+## Decision
+
+`GET /api/posts`, named `api.posts.index`, registered in `routes/api.ts` and
+served by `PostController.apiIndex`. It is public: it sits outside the `auth`
+alias group and stays a GET, so no session and no CSRF token are involved.
+
+- The envelope is `{ data, meta, links }`. `data` holds posts newest first
+  (`createdAt` descending, `id` breaking ties), ten per page, serialized by
+  the same `PostResource` the pages use — so a feed item carries exactly the
+  fields a page gets, including the `author` object.
+- `meta` is the framework `Paginator`'s: `currentPage`, `lastPage`,
+  `perPage`, `total`, `from`, `to`. When `?author=` filters the list, the
+  count reflects the filtered set.
+- `page` and `author` are declared on the route as `ApiListPostsQuerySchema`,
+  not only checked inside the handler. The router validates them before the
+  controller runs (422 with the usual `{ message, errors }` body), and
+  codegen publishes the contract, so the endpoint's accepted parameters are
+  visible to the project's route tooling.
+- The route also declares `resource: { data: [PostResource] }`, the same
+  type-level hint `posts.search` uses, which is what gives the generated
+  client a typed `json()`.
+
+The action counts and fetches in two queries instead of calling the query
+builder's `paginate()`, because that terminator drops eager-loaded relations
+and every feed item has to carry its author.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Posts are readable by anyone, including drafts-in-name-only — there is no
+published flag in the schema, so *every* post is public. Introducing one
+later means filtering here as well. The page size is fixed at ten: callers
+cannot ask for more, which keeps the endpoint cheap but means a client that
+wants a large window has to walk pages. Because the response is built from
+`PostResource`, adding a field to a page's post payload also adds it to the
+partner feed — check that before putting anything sensitive in the resource.
